AB  - Internet of Things (IoT) deployments increasingly rely on constrained devices with limited memory, processing capabilities, and energy resources, which limits the applicability of traditional transport-layer security mechanisms such as TLS and DTLS. This paper presents the design, implementation, and experimental validation of a secure IoT prototype that employs object-level protection for CoAP communication using OSCORE, with cryptographic material established through EDHOC. The proposed solution is implemented on an ESP32-based sensing node and a Raspberry Pi 400 gateway and evaluated under controlled laboratory conditions using real hardware. The experimental results confirm the feasibility of integrating EDHOC and OSCORE on resource-constrained devices and demonstrate reliable end-to-end message protection with robust behavior against common IoT security threats.  © 2026 IEEE.
