TI  - BLAKE: BLE-based lightweight authentication and key-exchange via PUF
PY  - 2026
T2  - Ad Hoc Networks
VL  - 193
C7  - 104370
DO  - 10.1016/j.adhoc.2026.104370
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046228452?origin=resultslist
AD  - Indian Institute of Technology Jammu, Jammu, 181221, India
AB  - Internet of Things (IoT) is rapidly transforming modern living by interconnecting billions of devices across diverse applications and enabling seamless data exchange. Several wireless sensor network (WSN) protocols, including Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE), Zigbee, and Z-Wave, support this ecosystem, with BLE emerging as the widely adopted standard due to its low cost, simplicity, and energy efficiency. However, existing BLE pairing mechanisms remain vulnerable to impersonation, man-in-the-middle (MITM), and eavesdropping attacks, especially in constrained smart-home deployments where devices often operate without strong physical protection or direct Internet connectivity, lacking robust authentication and public key validation. To address these challenges, we investigate lightweight PUF-assisted authentication for BLE deployments in which devices may rely on helper, fog or gateway devices for remote server connectivity. We first perform a detailed cryptanalysis of the PLAKE protocol, a lightweight PUF-based scheme for IoT authentication, and uncover several weaknesses, including impersonation, MITM, and session key disclosure vulnerabilities. Motivated by these weaknesses, we present BLAKE, a helper-assisted BLE-oriented realization of lightweight PUF-based authentication and key exchange that adapts a PLAKE-style design to BLE-constrained IoT deployment. In BLAKE, a BLE device communicates with a nearby client/helper device over BLE, while the helper relays protocol messages to a remote server, enabling secure authentication without requiring direct network connectivity at the BLE endpoint. The design retains lightweight XOR- and hash-based computation at the endpoint, while reducing verifier-side exposure by maintaining only a single active CRP per device at the server and avoiding dependence on a privileged helper/gateway that stores BLE-device-side secret material or CRP state. Symbolic security analysis using AVISPA, complemented by informal analysis under the stated threat model, provides evidence of resistance to the considered replay, impersonation, and session-key disclosure attacks in the modeled Dolev-Yao setting. A prototype implementation on ESP32, together with BLE-centric networking experiments covering multi-device operation and helper-impaired conditions, demonstrates low overhead and practical deployability for resource-constrained BLE-enabled IoT environments. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
