SP  - 1
EP  - 6
DO  - 10.1145/3785520.3785521
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031565998?origin=resultslist
AD  - Ho Chi Minh City University of Technology, Ho Chi Minh City, Viet Nam
AD  - University of Information Technology, Ho Chi Minh City, Viet Nam
AD  - Vietnam National University, Ho Chi Minh City, Viet Nam
AD  - International University, Ho Chi Minh City, Viet Nam
AB  - Emotion recognition from electroencephalogram (EEG) signals is increasingly used in wearable health monitoring and brain-computer interface (BCI) systems. Yet, EEG data contains highly sensitive information, posing privacy and security risks when transmitted from edge devices. This paper proposes a privacy-preserving embedded framework for real-time EEG signal processing and emotion classification, secured at the application layer using Ascon, the NIST-standard lightweight authenticated encryption. The system performs signal denoising using discrete wavelet transform and lightweight ICA, extracts time-frequency features and classifies emotional states using a quantized MLP model deployed on the Nordic nRF52840 microcontroller. To protect user data beyond BLE link-layer encryption, feature vectors are encrypted using Ascon-128 AEAD, ensuring confidentiality, integrity, and replay protection. Experiments on real EEG data show that the system achieves up to 88% accuracy, with encryption adding less than 5.5 ms latency and ∼2 mA power overhead. The Ascon module adds only 1.2 KB to firmware size, making it suitable for low-power wearables. Our work demonstrates that secure, real-time emotion inference is feasible on embedded edge devices without external servers or trusted BLE stacks. The framework establishes a baseline for secure AI deployment in resource-constrained health monitoring systems.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
