UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041868489?origin=resultslist
AD  - RNS Institute of Technology, Department of CSE-CY, Bengaluru, India
AB  - Contemporary smart-access systems often rely on mobile apps or tokens, yet many still transmit static codes or loosely protected messages, which makes them easy to clone or replay. This paper presents a SmartLock design that performs all security checks directly on the device, so it keeps working even without an Internet connection or a backend server.The lock uses Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) purely as a short-range link, and ties every unlock attempt to a fresh nonce and an Ed25519-signed Verifiable Presentation (VP) created on the user's phone. The phone encodes the nonce and an access claim into a compact JSON object, signs it with a key stored locally, and writes the result to a dedicated GATT characteristic. On the ESP32, the Monocypher library verifies the signature, checks that the nonce matches the one it issued, and confirms the claim before driving the relay. The W3C VP format was chosen over a plain signed message to provide a self-describing, standards-compliant credential envelope that supports future extensibility and decentralized identity integration. Measurements show that the full verification path completes in well under 20ms on the ESP32, providing a fast yet replay-resistant offline authentication flow suited to constrained IoT locks. © 2026 IEEE.
