TI  - Authentication Overhead in Post-Quantum IoT for Classical, Full-PQC and Hybrid TLS
PY  - 2026
T2  - Advances in Information, Electronic and Electrical Engineering, AIEEE 2026 - Proceedings of the 13th IEEE Workshop
DO  - 10.1109/AIEEE71113.2026.11577039
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044303808?origin=resultslist
AD  - Cyber Security Centre of Excellence, Kaunas University of Technology, Kaunas, Lithuania
AB  - The transition to post-quantum cryptography is particularly challenging for resource-constrained IoT devices, where improved cryptographic resilience must be balanced against latency, energy, and reliability constraints. This paper presents an empirical evaluation of TLS 1.3 authentication overhead on ESP32 under three key-exchange modes: classical X25519, full post-quantum ML-KEM-768, and hybrid X25519+ML-KEM-768. The study contributes the first direct large-scale comparison of these regimes on Xtensa-class hardware using wolfSSL and introduces IoT-QARS, an extension of the PAREK risk model that incorporates measured deployment overhead. Across 3,000 recorded sessions, including 2,938 successful handshakes, ML-KEM-768 reduced mean handshake latency by 43% and connection energy by 34% relative to X25519, while the hybrid mode increased latency and energy by only 11% and 7%, respectively. However, full PQC exhibited a 3.8% connectionfailure rate, likely caused by larger cryptographic payloads and buffer constraints. Applied to an industrial sensor profile, IoTQARS identifies hybrid deployment as the most practical nearterm migration option and highlights TCP-buffer tuning as a prerequisite for reliable full-PQC adoption. These results provide actionable guidance for post-quantum migration in embedded and industrial IoT environments.  © 2026 IEEE.
