AD  - Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Bannari Amman Institute of Technology, Tamil Nadu, Sathya Mangalam, Erode, India
AB  - The proliferation of Internet of Things (IoT) devices in smart homes necessitates secure, lightweight, and privacy-preserving authentication mechanisms. This paper presents a Physical Unclonable Function (PUF)-based secure and anonymous user authentication protocol specifically designed for resource-constrained smart home environments. The proposed scheme leverages PUF challenge response mechanisms for device-specific authentication and integrates dynamic pseudonym generation to ensure user anonymity. Implemented on an ESP32 IoT board with an Arbiter PUF, the protocol underwent formal verification using AVISPA and ProVerif, confirming resilience against cloning, impersonation, replay, eavesdropping, and man-in-the-middle attacks. Experimental results demonstrate high uniqueness (49.8%), reliability (>99%), and significant performance gains, with authentication times reduced by ~63% over ECC and ~ 82% over RSA, along with reduced memory and energy consumption. Deployment in a smart home testbed validated scalability to 50+ devices without performance degradation. The implementation confirms that PUF-based authentication offers a compelling balance between security, privacy, and efficiency for IoT smart homes. Future work will focus on enhancing PUF reliability under extreme environmental variations, integrating machine learning-resistant PUF architectures, and optimizing scalability for large-scale IoT ecosystems involving hundreds or thousands of devices. This research lays a robust foundation for next-generation secure and anonymous smart home authentication solutions. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
