AD  - Politeknik Siber Dan Sandi Negara, Cryptographic Engineering Program, Bogor, Indonesia
AD  - Politeknik Siber Dan Sandi Negara, Department of Cryptographic Engineering, Bogor, Indonesia
AB  - The widespread use of Internet of Things (IoT) devices demands lightweight ciphers that ensure strong security while remaining efficient in software. PRESENT, standardized as ISO/IEC 29192-2, is hardware-friendly but its linear key schedule and bitwise permutation are inefficient on 32/64-bit CPUs. This paper introduces PRESENT-H, a re-engineered variant that combines a key-dependent Feistel-ARX permutation layer with a non-linear key schedule (KSA-H). The design improves diffusion speed and reduces computational overhead, making it suitable for microcontroller-based IoT platforms. Experimental evaluation shows that PRESENT-H achieves faster avalanche propagation (t50 = 5 vs. t50 = 7), a higher diffusion curve area (1429.99 vs. 1374.17), and up to 3.8× performance improvement on ESP32. Security analysis of PRESENT-H indicates stronger resistance in early rounds, while PRESENT maintains a higher margin in full rounds. These results emphasize the trade-offs between hardware- and software-optimized ciphers, underscoring the need to align lightweight cryptography with its execution environment.  © 2025 IEEE.
