TI  - Key-Generation Latency and Retransmission Failure in Hybrid Post-Quantum TLS 1.3 Under Burst Packet Loss on Constrained IoT Devices
PY  - 2026
T2  - Learning and Analytics in Intelligent Systems
VL  - 63
SP  - 23
EP  - 33
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-29501-9_3
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042643931?origin=resultslist
AD  - Dr. B.R. Ambedkar National Institute of Technology, Punjab, Jalandhar, India
AB  - As the industry transitions to quantum-safe cryptography, hybrid post-quantum algorithms are being deployed on constrained IoT devices that communicate over lossy wireless links. An IoT device that spends a longer time generating a key share before transmitting its first packet silently exhausts the TCP retransmission budget, causing handshake failures that appear to be network errors but are caused by local computation. We present an empirical study of five TLS 1.3 key-exchange algorithms on an ESP32 across 16,000 trials spanning eight network scenarios, including a correlated burst-loss model based on the Gilbert-Elliott channel. Client-side key-generation time emerges as the dominant predictor of timeout-induced failure, with a Pearson correlation of 0.97 against the retransmission proxy rate. P-384+ML-KEM-1024 (1255 ms key generation time) fails the retransmission budget in 91 to 98% of trials, while X25519+ML-KEM-768 and ML-KEM-512 remain reliable across all scenarios. These results establish a practical deployment rule: on constrained devices, the classical component of a hybrid scheme matters more than the post-quantum parameter set, and key-generation time is a hardware-measurable pre-filter for algorithm selection before a single packet is sent. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
