SP  - 46
EP  - 55
DO  - 10.1109/DCOSS-IoT69657.2026.00013
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049676259?origin=resultslist
AD  - Kuwait University, College of Engineering & Petroleum, Computer Engineering Department, Kuwait City, Kuwait
AB  - In many mission-critical settings, securing distributed IoT fleets demands unlinkable authorization. This allows multiple authorized controllers to command devices without linking commands to specific identities, thus preventing operator attribution if a device is captured. While traditional mutual authentication mechanisms exposes identity, privacy-preserving schemes such as ring or group signatures often impose prohibitive memory overheads on constrained hardware. This work presents a lightweight, privacy-preserving, end-to-end implementation of a zkSNARK-based anonymous command authorization framework optimized for commodity microcontrollers such as the ESP32 and ESP32-S3. The system enables verifiable yet anonymous command transmission, allowing an IoT node to confirm that a command originates from an authorized controller without revealing the controller's identity or any sensitive metadata. Unlike previous zero-knowledge-based implementations that rely on powerful Single Board Computers (SBCs), we propose a resource-asymmetric architecture where computationally intensive proof generation is offloaded to capable controllers, while verification is made efficient for resource-constrained devices using Groth16 zero-knowledge proofs combined with Merkle tree membership proofs. A prototype implementation on ESP32-S3 microcontrollers (240 MHz, 512 KB RAM) achieves on-device proof verification in approximately 2.5 seconds with 9,948 R1CS constraints, yielding asymptotically constant verifier storage compared to the linear growth of traditional ring signature schemes (e.g., 32 B versus 8 KB, a ×256 reduction in storage for 256 authorized controllers), constant-time verification independent of the authorized set size, and minimal energy consumption (1.74 J per proof). These results demonstrate our scheme's applicability to privacy-preserving command authorization for real-time control of autonomous systems. © 2026 IEEE.
