AD  - University of Texas at Dallas, Department of Computer Science, Richardson, TX, United States
AB  - Password-based authentication mechanisms remain vulnerable to phishing, credential reuse, and centralized data breaches. While biometric authentication improves usability and security, most existing solutions rely on cloud-based inference and server-side biometric storage, raising privacy, latency, and scalability concerns. In this paper, we present a fully passwordless web authentication system enabled by Tiny Machine Learning (TinyML), where biometric inference is performed entirely on-device using a resource-constrained microcontroller. Our system deploys a quantized face recognition model on an ESP32-CAM, enabling real-time identity verification without transmitting raw biometric data to the cloud. Upon successful local authentication, the edge device securely triggers a JSON Web Token (JWT)-based session establishment with a web backend, enabling seamless access to protected web resources. We evaluate the proposed system in terms of inference latency, memory footprint, authentication accuracy, and end-to-end authentication delay. Experimental results demonstrate that secure, privacy-preserving web authentication can be achieved using on-device TinyML inference with minimal computational and memory overhead, making the approach suitable for embedded and IoT deployments.  © 2026 IEEE.
