AB  - In the era of digital healthcare, the secure transmission and storage of medical images are paramount to preserving patient privacy and complying with data protection regulations. This project presents a lightweight and efficient encryption framework designed for Internet of Things (IoT)devices, specifically the ESP32 microcontroller. The proposed method utilises high-speed scrambling and pixel-adaptive diffusion techniques to effectively encrypt medical images without compromising performance on resource-constrained hardware. Medical images are first converted into binary data and transferred to the ESP32 using the SPI Flash File System (SPIFFS). Once on the device, the data undergoes a two-phase encryption process involving rapid pixel scrambling followed by adaptive diffusion based on pixel intensity and neighbourhood dynamics. The encrypted binary output is then reconstructed into an image format suitable for secure storage and transmission. This work highlights the feasibility of on-device medical image encryption and its relevance for enhancing data security in modern telemedicine applications. © 2026 Institute of Physics Publishing. All rights reserved.
