AB  - This paper presents a model for cryptographic key generation based on ECG-fingerprint, designed for wearable biomedical systems with real-time edge processing of biosignals. The proposed architecture integrates a physiological biometric source with lightweight symmetric encryption algorithms, eliminating the need to store or transmit raw biosignals. The system performs the entire processing cycle from ECG acquisition to biometric vector extraction, key generation, and encryption - locally on edge devices such as STM32 or ESP32-S3 microcontrollers. Feature vectors are transformed into stable binary fingerprints using fuzzy correction mechanisms to ensure key reproducibility under physiological changes, noise, and motion artifacts. The keys are integrated into ASCON, TinyJAMBU, and PHOTON-Beetle lightweight cryptographic protocols. Experimental evaluation demonstrated high entropy, strong inter-user differentiation, and real-time performance with minimal power and memory consumption. The approach is robust against spoofing, replay, and template inversion attacks, making it suitable for secure data exchange in personalized cardiomonitoring and medical IoT scenarios. © 2026 IEEE.
