AD  - St. Joseph's College of Engineering, Department Of ECE, Chennai, India
AB  - The high cost and limited availability of conventional oscilloscopes severely restrict hands-on training and prototyping experiments for students, hobbyists, and resource-constrained environments. This work presents the design and implementation of a low-cost Wi-Fi-based smartphone oscilloscope using the ESP32 microcontroller, which allows real-time remote observation of signals and cloud-based logging of data. The system is capable of recording analog signals using the onboard Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) of the ESP32, applying edge-level Kalman filtering to remove noise, and sending the filtered data via Wi-Fi to a web interface on smartphones. Secure data logging is also facilitated by employing the Message Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT) protocol, thus allowing remote observation and long-term storage of data. The main objective is to offer a low-cost, easy-to-use, and portable instrument for electronics diagnostics and education. The contribution of the work lies in the integration of real-time Kalman-based filtering, software-timed dual-channel synchronization, and encrypted cloud logging using MQTT in a smartphone-compatible platform based on the ESP32.  © 2025 IEEE.
