AB  - Biometric authentication using physiological signals has gained attention for its security and robustness. This study presents a TinyML based authentication system leveraging Photoplethysmography (PPG) signals for real-time, edge-based identity verification. We explored various segmentation techniques, including windowing, peak-to-peak segmentation, and Pulse Segmentation to optimize feature extraction. The BioSec.Lab PPG Dataset was utilized alongside time domain, spectral and spectrogram-based feature extraction to enhance classification accuracy. Multiple deep learning architectures - including Deep Neural Network (DNN), 1D-Convolutional Neural Network (1D-CNN), and 2D-CNN, were evaluated for PPG-based biometric authentication. Our model achieved a peak accuracy of 96.64% for 30 individuals. The model is optimized for deployment on wearable and edge devices, requiring only a single point of contact for authentication. Finally, model performance was evaluated on the Espressif ESP-EYE (ESP32, 240 MHz), highlighting the potential of TinyML powered biometric authentication, offering a lightweight, secure, and real-time solution for identity verification on resource constrained devices. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
