TI  - Reducing Post-Quantum Cryptography Overhead in IoT Networks Using an Epoch-Based Compression Approach
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Frontiers of Engineering and Emerging Technologies, FET 2026
DO  - 10.1109/FET68771.2026.11601673
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046058731?origin=resultslist
AD  - National School of Arts and Crafts, Computer Engineering Department, Casablanca, Morocco
AB  - Post-quantum cryptography (PQC) protects against future quantum attacks but introduces significant bandwidth overhead for resource-constrained Internet of Things (IoT) devices. This paper presents an epoch-based architecture combining LZ4 compression with ML-KEM-512 key encapsulation to reduce transmission costs in bandwidth-limited IoT networks. Hardware benchmarks on ESP32-DevKitC demonstrate that ML-KEM-512 key operations complete in under 20 ms total, with LZ4 compression achieving 1.04-2.34× ratios on realistic IoT payloads in under 0.5 ms. At N=100 messages per epoch, the PQC bandwidth gap relative to compressed classical ECDH narrows from 478% down to 8.3%, and the amortized ML-KEM-512 overhead reduces to 15.68 bytes per message. Although the absolute PQC penalty remains a fixed 1,440 bytes per epoch when comparing equal compression baselines, session batching makes this overhead practically negligible. A device tier taxonomy maps RFC 7228 device classes to PQC capability requirements. The results indicate that compression-assisted PQC is viable for IoT deployments where message batching is feasible. © 2026 IEEE.
