AD  - Western Washington University, Department of Computer Science, Bellingham, United States
AB  - The Internet of Things (IoT) faces myriad threats, serves countless users, and is largely populated by computationally constrained devices that necessitate lightweight security solutions. Physically Unclonable Functions (PUFs) offer a promising security primitive that requires minimal computation overhead, consumes little power, and naturally resists physical tampering. In this work, we introduce SPEAK (Secure PUF-Enabled Authentication and Key-Exchange), a novel protocol leveraging TRNG-powered random obfuscation and physically unclonable functions (PUFs) to achieve efficient key exchange and built-in authentication without the need for conventional encryption or hashing. We detail the implementation of SPEAK on an ESP32-C3 microcontroller, utilizing a 128-bit XOR-2 MemPUF, and evaluate its practical deployment over MQTT. Our security analysis demonstrates SPEAK's resilience against a range of attack vectors, including replay attacks, man-in-the-middle (MITM) attacks, and denial of service attacks. Furthermore, we conduct extensive machine learning-based modeling attacks, including those employing CMA-ES metaheuristics, and show that SPEAK maintains robust security against such adversaries. Experimental results reveal that SPEAK achieves significantly lower latency and improved throughput compared to SSL/TLS-based solutions, while remaining well-suited for ultra-lightweight IoT endpoints. These findings position SPEAK as a practical and scalable protocol for secure communication in next-generation IoT environments.  © 2025 IEEE.
