AD  - Amrita School of Computing, Amrita Vishwa Vidyapeetham, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Bengaluru, India
AB  - Smell-based authentication is a novel alternative to conventional security mechanisms. However, practical implementations of embedded security and biometric authentication remain limited within this domain. While research into electronic noses is being pursued, most of the existing solutions are based on complex, high-cost instrumentation. There is thus a definite need for developing portable, low-cost, and real-time systems. In this regard, this work proposes OSAS- the Olfactory Sensing Authentication System, a robust embedded framework for odor recognition based on low-cost gas sensors and ondevice machine learning. The OSAS system integrates MQ-2, MQ-3, and MQ-135 gas sensors with an ESP32-S3 microcontroller in an attempt to capture the volatile chemical signature that comes out of different perfume samples. Comprehensive preprocessing steps like temperature-humidity compensation, signal smoothing, environmental normalization, and automated removal of residual odor interference have been done to enhance the quality of the signal. Statistical features will be extracted from the Flatten block and then classification using a Fully Connected Neural Network is done, which has been deployed on the microcontroller. Experimental results demonstrate the OSAS system's effectiveness with a classification accuracy of 92.2%. An OLED display is used to enhance interpretability, while a servo actuator, buck converter, and automated fan mechanism support operational reliability. These findings demonstrate the prospect of a VOC(Volatile Organic Compounds)-based authentication technique as a novel and promising non-intrusive, hard-to-forge, and efficient approach to embedded security. © 2026 IEEE.
