TI  - Application of the Ascon-Aead128 Algorithm for IoT Data Security
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 11th International Conference on Applying New Technology in Green Buildings, ATiGB 2026
SP  - 1233
EP  - 1238
DO  - 10.1109/ATiGB70203.2026.11628316
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048715528?origin=resultslist
AD  - University of Technology and Education, The University of Danang, Viet Nam
AB  - Ensuring data security and integrity in resourceconstrained embedded systems remains a significant challenge for conventional cryptographic algorithms due to their high computational and energy costs. Lightweight cryptography (LWC) addresses this issue by optimizing performance for constrained hardware platforms. Among LWC candidates, Ascon-AEAD128, selected as a standard by NIST [1], stands out due to its authenticated encryption with associated data (AEAD) scheme, simple permutation-based structure, and high efficiency in both hardware and software implementations. This paper presents the implementation and performance evaluation of Ascon-AEAD128 on two heterogeneous hardware platforms, namely ESP32-C6 and Raspberry Pi 4, representing different classes of embedded devices. Performance is analyzed in terms of throughput (MB/s) and computational cost (cycles/byte) across multiple payload sizes. Experimental results show that, for a 16-byte payload, the throughput reaches 2.449 MB/s (612.48 cycles/byte) on Raspberry Pi 4 and 0.504 MB/s (317.23 cycles/byte) on ESP32-C6. When the payload increases to 128 bytes, the performance improves to 8.834 MB/s (170.41 cycles/byte) and 1.541 MB/s (103.86 cycles/byte), respectively. In addition, an end-to-end LoRa transmission test using an 11-byte weather data payload was conducted. A total of 10,000 encrypted packets were transmitted from the ESP32-C6 node to the Raspberry Pi 4 gateway, with 0 authentication failures and 0 payload recovery errors observed. These results demonstrate that Ascon-AEAD128 is a suitable option for secure data transmission under the tested LoRa-based IoT conditions in resource-constrained embedded systems.  © 2026 IEEE.
