AD  - Graphic Era Hill University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Haldwani, India
AD  - Iftm University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Moradabad, India
AB  - The proliferation of heterogeneous and resource constrained Internet of Things (IoT) devices has intensified the need for lightweight, tamper-proof, and decentralized intrusion detection mechanisms. This paper proposes a lightweight intrusion detection framework that integrates Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM)-based statistical anomaly detection with a permissioned Ethereum blockchain operating under the Dele gated Byzantine Fault Tolerance (dBFT) consensus mechanism. The system architecture comprises local intrusion detection agents that monitor network traffic at the device level, extract statistical features in real time, and compute anomaly scores via a probabilistic GMM. Upon detecting persistent anomalies, agents encapsulate cryptographically signed alerts containing hashed evidence and severity levels. These alerts are submitted to the blockchain layer, where Solidity-based smart contracts automate alert verification, quorum based consensus, and dynamic trust score management. The framework was evaluated using the TON-IoT dataset in a hybrid testbed of Raspberry Pi 4B and ESP32 devices. Experimental results demonstrate a detection accuracy of 97.8%, with a precision of 96.9%, recall of 98.6%, F1-score of 97.7%, and an average detection latency of 230 ms. Blockchain performance analysis confirms low transaction finality (2-4s) and minimal gas consumption for contract functions, validating the feasibility of deploying the framework in real-world IoT networks.  © 2025 IEEE.
