TI  - A Lightweight Hybrid Encryption Architecture for High-Telemetry Constrained IoT Devices
PY  - 2026
T2  - SIU 2026 - 34th Signal Processing and Communications Applications Conference, Conference Proceedings
DO  - 10.1109/SIU71813.2026.11636899
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048766638?origin=resultslist
AD  - National University of Sciences and Technology, School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science, Islamabad, Pakistan
AB  - Constrained IoT devices producing high-frequency telemetry need authenticated encryption and key rotation but lack resources for full session-negotiation. Using static AES-GCM avoids protocol overhead but risks traffic exposure in the event of key compromise. We propose hybrid secure IoT communication (H-SIC) that offers lightweight key rotation without a session-negotiation stack. Bulk payload encryption employs hardware AES-128-GCM, while session-key derivation and key encapsulation operate in CPU software. A dual-tag binding authenticates keys and payloads separately, while a caching mechanism spreads out the cost of key wrapping over each packet epoch. It also prevents contention on the hardware cryptographic peripheral by limiting key management to software. Evaluation on ESP32 reveals 46 to 86 percent per-packet latency reduction via caching, a tunable security-performance trade-off, and a resource cost of only 15 KB flash and 200 B heap over raw AES-GCM. © 2026 IEEE.
