AD  - Vit -AP, Amaravathi, India
AD  - Vit -AP, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Amaravathi, India
AB  - Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) or the so-called drones increasingly use wireless protocols like Wi-Fi for remote control due to their accessibility and ease of deployment. This, however, exposes the control channel to bad security attacks like spoofing, command injection, and replay attacks. Though encryption is commonly used for protecting the communication, in this work, we present a different approach: protecting the control of a drone by authenticating each command so that only commands from a valid controller are executed. We use a lightweight, publickey cryptography-based scheme on top of the Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) to authenticate one-way transmissions of commands. To protect against replay attacks without relying on a persistent connection, a timestamp-based verification mechanism is employed. Implemented on an ESP32 drone(quadcopter), our system demonstrates that command level authentication can effectively prevent unauthorized access and tampering, yet remain effective on resource constrained embedded systems. We compare the proposed method to AESGCM, RSA 2048, and unencrypted UDP, measuring latency and performance. The results verify that drone command authentication is an efficient, scalable, and secure control solution where authentication, rather than traditional encryption, is an achievable paradigm for securing UAV control systems.  © 2025 IEEE.
