AB  - The rapid growth of Internet of Things devices has created a demand for intelligent, real-time data processing. However, running ML models directly on resource-constrained devices or relying on distant cloud servers brings major issues like delays, privacy risks, and energy consumption. This paper introduces FogGuard, a middleware system designed to facilitate secure and efficient ML execution on local edge nodes within an IoT environment. The FogGuard system employs a lightweight architecture that intercepts sensor data, performs localized ML-based anomaly detection, and ensures secure communication between devices and the application layer. The methodology involved a quantitative experimental study where FogGuard was implemented using Raspberry Pi as an edge node and ESP32 devices as sensors. Performance was evaluated against a traditional cloud-centric model. The results demonstrate that FogGuard reduces the average end-to-end latency for threat detection by 72% (from 450ms to 125ms) and decreases device power consumption by 18% due to reduced data transmission. Furthermore, the embedded ML model successfully identified 96.5% of simulated injection attacks. This study concludes that edge-based middleware like FogGuard presents a viable and highly efficient solution for deploying secure, intelligent applications in latency-sensitive and privacy-conscious IoT ecosystems.  © 2025 IEEE.
