AB  - This study investigates the feasibility and performance impact of implementing WireGuard VPN on ESP32-based IoT devices, focusing on security, resource utilization, and network efficiency. Using the ESP-IDF v4.4 development framework and FreeRTOS operating system, performance metrics were evaluated over 10 data transmission cycles for both VPN-enabled and non-VPN configurations. Measurements were obtained via internal APIs for memory profiling, latency measurement, and CPU utilization tracking. Results indicate that WireGuard increases heap memory usage by 13.7%, static DRAM usage by 4.2% (from 30,520 bytes to 31,804 bytes), and flash memory usage by 5.2% (from 679,255 bytes to 714,603 bytes), all within ESP32's operational limits. CPU utilization increased by 144.4% in relative terms but remained below 1% in absolute values, demonstrating the efficiency of WireGuard implementation. Latency rose from 23.1 ms to 665.0 ms due to encryption and tunneling overhead but remained acceptable for IoT applications transmitting data periodically. Wireshark analysis confirmed that WireGuard effectively encrypts data, preventing payload interception, with a modest packet overhead of 40-60 bytes. efficient communication. This study highlights WireGuard's viability for resource-constrained IoT environments, demonstrating robust security with minimal performance impact. Future work will explore scalability in larger IoT ecosystems and performance under dynamic network conditions.  © 2025 IEEE.
