AB  - This study proposes a lightweight secure communication protocol for Internet of Things operating in resource-constrained environments. The protocol introduces a wired PIN-based pre-authentication phase to verify device identity, followed by a wireless handshake that uses elliptic curve Diffie–Hellman to establish a shared session key. To ensure confidentiality, a modified version of the Spritz stream cipher is used to encrypt messages between devices. The protocol was implemented using Python and tested with two simulated ESP32 devices in Wokwi, with message transmission handled via the HiveMQ MQTT broker. Results demonstrate successful authentication, key agreement, and encrypted data exchange, supporting the protocol’s effectiveness in lightweight, real-time IoT systems. ©2025 IEEE.
