TI  - CIP-BaseX: A Lightweight RFC 4648-Compatible Obfuscation Layer Against IoT Payload Fingerprinting
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Internet of Things Journal
VL  - 13
IS  - 13
SP  - 28573
EP  - 28586
DO  - 10.1109/JIOT.2026.3684991
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036101247?origin=resultslist
AD  - China University of Technology, Department of Computer Science and Information Engineering, Taipei, 116, Taiwan
AB  - Standard Base16/32/64 encodings (RFC 4648) are fundamental to modern data transmission but rely on static lookup tables, producing deterministic outputs that expose encrypted traffic to payload fingerprinting and statistical analysis. To mitigate these risks in resource-constrained Internet of Things (IoT) environments, this research introduces Chaos-Index-Permuted BaseX (CIP-BaseX), a lightweight traffic obfuscation scheme that leverages deterministic chaos to inject seed-driven variability into standard encodings. Chaotic maps (e.g., Logistic, Tent, and Hénon) are utilized to generate dynamic sequences, which are mapped to discrete indices via a rank-based sorting algorithm. This process creates a dynamically permuted alphabet that remains 100% format-compatible with RFC 4648, preserving character sets and padding rules. Experimental validation on ESP32-S3 and RP2040 microcontrollers demonstrates that the O(n \log n) permutation overhead is negligible, with index generation times ranging from 51 to 345μ s on ESP32-S3. Statistical analysis confirms strong dispersion, achieving near-maximal Hamming distance (HD) (e.g., HD ≥ 59 for Base64) and near-random rank correlation (τ ≈0 ) against standard tables. CIP-BaseX does not provide cryptographic confidentiality and is not intended to replace encryption; rather, it operates as a format-preserving variability layer to disrupt deterministic BaseX fingerprints. Unlike heavy cryptosystems, CIP-BaseX is defined as a dynamic representation layer within encrypt-then-encode pipelines, effectively thwarting static pattern recognition in text-based communication channels.  © 2014 IEEE.
