AB  - The growing proliferation of the Internet of Things (IoT) necessitates lightweight cryptographic algorithms that balance security and efficiency f or r esource-constrained environments. Traditional cryptographic methods are often too resource-intensive for devices with limited processing power, memory, and energy capacity. Lightweight cryptography (LWC) addresses this by providing optimized solutions with minimal hardware over-head. One such candidate, the New Lightweight Cryptographic Algorithm (NLCA), a 128-bit symmetric block cipher. This paper introduces a modified version of NLCA, called ModNLCA, which replaces the original F-function with a dynamic S-Box generated from the secret key and round constants. This modification enhances resistance to linear attacks while potentially reducing computational complexity. The algorithm is implemented on ESP32 microcontrollers to simulate real-world IoT scenarios, where encryption and decryption processes are distributed across nodes via Wi-Fi communication. Experimental results show that while ModNLCA is slightly slower than PRESENT at small data sizes (e.g., 32 bits), it outperforms both NLCA and PRESENT significantly at l arger sizes. At 256 bits, ModNLCA a chieves up to 53% faster encryption and 38% faster decryption compared to PRESENT, while also using memory more efficiently. Moreover, ModNLCA demonstrates a strong diffusion effect, achieving an avalanche effect rate of 25.34 %. These findings s uggest that ModNLCA is a promising candidate for secure and efficient encryption in resource-constrained systems.  © 2025 IEEE.
