AD  - National Cyber and Crypto Polytechnic, Bogor, Indonesia
AB  - Internet of Things (IoT) devices in mission-critical domains, including industrial SCADA systems, energy infrastructure, and physical security devices, often operate in air-gapped environments to minimize exposure to remote attacks. In such conditions, firmware updates must be conducted through local physical channels, typically using the UART serial interface. However, UART transmits data in plaintext by default, making it susceptible to eavesdropping, malicious firmware injection, and supply chain manipulation. This work addresses a specific gap in the security architecture of devices that operate without network connectivity by proposing and evaluating the Application-Layer UART Firmware Update (AL-UFU) framework, which integrates ASCON-128, the official NIST 2023 Lightweight Cryptography (LWC) standard, using Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data (AEAD) mode on the ESP32 platform. The system operates fully offline and does not rely on external infrastructure. Quantitative evaluation over 100 iterations on real ESP32 hardware shows that the framework introduces minimal memory overhead, requiring 7.48 KB of Flash (0.58%) and 2.03 KB of SRAM (0.63%); the cryptographic processing adds an average overhead of 7.648 ms per packet (8.03%) with a sustained throughput of 9.719 KB/s; and all evaluated active attack vectors, including invalid key usage, MITM/bit-flip attacks, and associated data (AD) tampering, are immediately rejected at the first chunk under the assumed adversary model, ensuring that no unauthenticated firmware is written to Flash memory. Finally, the use of a monolithic binary format (.bin) ensures atomic updates and effectively prevents partial-update attacks. © 2026 IEEE.
