AB  - We propose a lightweight end-to-end authentication and key-exchange protocol for resource-constrained IoT devices that avoids computationally expensive cryptographic operations by leveraging Physically Unclonable Functions (PUFs) and employs a lightweight Fuzzy Extractor (FE) to reliably handle PUF noise. The protocol relies exclusively on lightweight hash, XOR, and symmetric encryption/decryption operations. Moreover, the proposed protocol mitigates the storage overhead by avoiding the maintenance of large CRP repositories at the server. Security properties are formally verified using the scyther tool. A prototype implementation using two ESP32-based IoT nodes and a server further demonstrates its practicality for real-world deployments. Finally, benchmarking against state-of-the-art schemes indicates that the proposed protocol incurs comparatively low computation time and communication cost, highlighting its efficiency and lightweight nature. © 2009-2012 IEEE.
