AB  - This study introduces a simple and safe motion control system based on resource-constrained Internet of Things hardware. The system uses an ESP32 microcontroller with an MPU6050 motion sensor to record roll and pitch movements, which are then mapped to forward, backward, left, and right driving instructions. AES-GCM encryption is used to protect motion commands, which provide confidentiality and integrity. It uses a dual-channel design in which commands are sent by an ESP32 over an NRF24L01 module to an Arduino Nano with an L298N motor driver to actuate the motor with low latency. MQTT broadcasts encrypts telemetry to record in the cloud server. The MQTT data are recorded in a MySQL database and visualized on Grafana dashboards to enable real-time telemetry. The experimental validation demonstrates that AES-GCM has a significant overhead (approximately 0.35 ms encryption and 0.40 ms decryption) compared to normal flow. This maintains the MQTT transmission time under 5-8 ms and RF actuation under 2 ms to maintain real-time responsiveness. The proposed framework shows that it is possible to implement robust, lightweight security into motion-driven autonomous control without affecting performance, thereby proposing a scalable approach to smart mobility and IoT applications. © 2025 IEEE.
