TI  - A Hybrid Monitoring Framework for Anomaly Detection in Smart Home IoT Devices
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of the 18th IEEE/ACM International Conference on Utility and Cloud Computing, UCC 2025
C7  - 61
DO  - 10.1145/3773274.3774927
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105027223852?origin=resultslist
AD  - Newcastle University, Newcastle upon Tyne, United Kingdom
AB  - The rapid expansion of smart home ecosystems has led to widespread deployment of Internet of Things (IoT) devices, many of which are built on resource-constrained system-on-chip (SoC). While these devices offer significant convenience and automation, their limited internal visibility and lack of strong built-in security mechanisms make them vulnerable to a range of cyber threats. Existing monitoring approaches primarily rely on network-level analysis, which, although non-intrusive, has become increasingly insufficient due to the adoption of encryption protocols and emerging standards like Matter that hide payload content. Moreover, network-only methods are unable to detect internal device anomalies, such as memory leaks, task scheduling issues, or abnormal system usage that often precede visible attacks. In this paper, we propose a hybrid monitoring framework that integrates network-level traffic analysis with device-level telemetry to enable more comprehensive and resilient anomaly detection. Our evaluation leverages a combination of commercial smart home devices, including Matter-enabled and Tasmota-based bulbs and switches, and a custom ESP32-based emulator that provides fine-grained access to internal system metrics. We evaluate these devices under realistic attack scenarios, including Denial-of-Service (DoS) and Man-in-the-Middle (MitM), and demonstrate that our hybrid monitoring approach can detect anomalies effectively. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
