AD  - Department of Bachelor of Computer Applications (BCA), Jims Engineering Management Technical Campus, Uttar Pradesh, Greater Noida, India
AD  - Department of Information Technology and Services, Micron Technology, Boise, ID, United States
AB  - The Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) is the collection of medical devices and applications that connect to healthcare IT systems through computer networks. Medical devices equipped with Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, cellular networks, Ethernet, and low-power wide-area networks (LPWANs) like LoRa and NB-IoT allow the machine-to-machine communication that is the basis of IoMT. IoMT devices link to cloud platforms such as Amazon Web Services (AWS), on which captured data can be stored and analyzed. IoMT is also known as healthcare IoT. As is the case with the latest Internet of Things (IoT), there are now more possible applications of IoMT than before. Consumers now can connect mobile devices built with near-field communication (NFC) radio frequency identification (RFID) tags and other connecting sensors that allow the devices to share information with IT systems. The IoT devices are typically connected to the Internet via an Internet protocol (IP) network. However, devices such as bluetooth and RFID allow IoT devices to connect locally. As far as the IoT communication protocols or technologies are concerned, a mix of both IP and non-IP networks can be considered depending on usage. IoT protocols and standards will also be covered, like HTTP, long-range wide area networks, bluetooth, ZigBee, message queue telemetry transport (MQTT), and constrained application protocol (CoAP). The motivation of lightweight cryptography is to use less memory, fewer computing resources, and less power supply to provide security solutions that can work over resource-limited devices. Lightweight cryptography is expected to be simpler and faster than conventional cryptography. This chapter covers all technological issues in extracting that data from IoMT, storing medical records, and securing medical IoT, where blockchains will provide secured and authenticated transfers of patient personal data. © 2026 The Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers, Inc.
