AB  - Resource-constrained terminals suffer noticeable access latency when post-quantum key establishment is placed on every connection. This paper presents a low-latency hybrid postquantum secure access protocol for terminals connected to a fixed trusted gateway. The full handshake combines ephemeral P-256 ECDH, ML-KEM-512, and a device pre-shared key through HKDF-SHA256, while the resumption path derives a ticketindexed PSK and HMAC-SHA256 binder from the previous hybrid root state. The paper defines an active adversary model, gives reduction-style arguments, and verifies the core logic in ProVerif. On an ESP32-S3/Linux-gateway testbed, 300-round measurements show 355.95 ms average full-handshake latency and 41.73 ms average resumption latency, with only 304 B online traffic during resumption. Confidence intervals, bootstrap P95 intervals, phase decomposition, and rank-sum tests show that the improvement is stable rather than a single-sample artifact. © 2026 IEEE.
