AB  - The Internet of Things (IoT) is quickly becoming a common technology that will improve people's lives by integrating into many parts of modern life and helping share information across platforms. Device Authentication and Key Exchange are significant challenges for the IoT, which is crucial for everyday living. Many IoT sensor devices are placed in unprotected environments, making them vulnerable to physical attacks and common security risks because they have limited resources and weak self-protection abilities. The large computing power needed for cryptographic processes, along with heavy message transmission during Authentication, makes existing methods like Cryptography and Encryption unsuitable for these resource-constrained devices. Physical Unclonable Function (PUF) offers a practical and cost-effective security solution compared to typically complex cryptosystems like Cryptography and Encryption. PUF provides a unique signature that cannot be copied and is sensitive to tampering, created from the variability of the manufacturing process. In this study, we comparing the existing PUF's architecture. And in that we are SRAM PUF which we implemented on ESP32 target board. In the second work, in order to testing CRPs against ML attacks we used pypuf framework module to evaluate PUF security along with the network performance metrics such as reliability, randomness and uniqueness. © 2025 IEEE.
