AD  - Curtin University Malaysia, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Sarawak, Miri, 98009, Malaysia
AB  - Message queuing telemetry transport (MQTT) is widely used for publish-subscribe communication in the Internet of Things (IoT). However, MQTT requires a broker that is typically hosted on cloud servers or devices with greater computational resources, such as Raspberry Pi systems or computers. To address this limitation, WebSocket queuing telemetry transport (WQTT) is introduced in this article as a lightweight communication framework that leverages WebSockets while preserving the publish-subscribe messaging model. Unlike MQTT, WQTT enables resource-constrained microcontrollers such as the ESP32 to operate as a broker. WQTT implements application-layer retransmission for reliable message delivery, AES-GCM encryption for message confidentiality, integrity, and replay protection, and runtime message retention to ensure newly subscribed clients receive the latest topic messages. In addition, the protocol supports automatic client reconnection and topic resubscription following broker crashes or client disconnections, ensuring reliable recovery in unstable network conditions. This article presents the architecture of WQTT and makes the source code publicly available. Four test scenarios were conducted to evaluate WQTT's performance on ESP32 devices acting as both broker and clients: 1) publish load test; 2) unsubscription test; 3) broker crash recovery; and 4) client crash recovery. Results demonstrate 99.6% subscription accuracy and packet delivery ratio (PDR) across all scenarios, with average round-trip times (RTTs) ranging from 250 to 300 ms depending on network load.  © 2014 IEEE.
