AD  - Judson University, Elgin, IL, United States
AD  - Embedded Software, Gujarat, Ahmedabad, India
AB  - Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) Internet of Things (IoT) devices are widely deployed in consumer, industrial, and sensing applications where firmware integrity is critical to device security. Secure boot is a recognized foundational defense against firmware tampering, yet empirical performance data for resource-constrained BLE microcontroller-class devices remains limited. The EU Cyber Resilience Act (Regulation EU 2024/2847) and EU Radio Equipment Directive (EU RED) now require authenticated firmware update mechanisms for connected devices sold in Europe. Manufacturers of BLE products must document compliance evidence as part of conformity assessment. We evaluate MCUboot-based secure boot on the Nordic nRF52840 under the nRF Connect SDK to address this need. Three configurations are evaluated: an unsigned baseline (C1), RSA-2048 signature verification without encryption (C2), and RSA-2048 signature verification with AES-128 firmware encryption (C3). Flash consumption across configurations ranges from 54.05% to 98.61% of the 48 KB MCUboot partition, with bootloader execution times of 379 ms, 498 ms, and 570 ms respectively. All configurations were validated against firmware tampering, wrong-key, and downgrade attack scenarios. Results are mapped against CRA Annex I and EU RED Article 3(3) obligations, providing, to the authors’ knowledge, the first empirical overhead dataset and regulatory compliance guidance for MCUboot on a production BLE SoC. © 2026 IEEE.
