AB  - Lightweight cryptographic algorithms are essential for securing communication in resource-constrained environments such as Internet of Things (IoT) devices, RFID tags, and embedded systems. This paper proposes a deterministic hybrid key-wrapping scheme that integrates the PRESENT ultra-lightweight block cipher and the Salsa20 stream cipher to provide layered message protection. In the proposed scheme, PRESENT encrypts the session key prior to its use in Salsa20 encryption, adding a key-protection layer without introducing the computational overhead of the chaotic systems used in prior PRESENT and Salsa20 hybrid designs. The scheme is implemented in portable C and empirically evaluated on physical IoT development boards spanning three architecture classes: an 8-bit ATmega328P (16 MHz), a 32-bit ARM Cortex-M4 (STM32F411, 96 MHz), and a 32-bit Xtensa ESP32 (240 MHz). Performance, memory footprint, and per-byte cost are reported as measured values rather than analytical projections. The measured results reveal that the PRESENT key-wrapping cost is effectively constant per session (approximately 68.7 ms on the ATmega328P, 0.87 ms on the STM32F411, and 0.65 ms on the ESP32), so its relative overhead depends strongly on payload size, dominating short-message workloads on the 8-bit device while becoming negligible (below 3%) for larger payloads on the 32-bit devices. A key-management flow validation on a virtualized multi-node testbed confirms correct end-to-end wrap, transmit, unwrap, and decrypt operation across five concurrent nodes. The proposed approach offers a simpler and more deterministic alternative to chaos-based hybrid designs while remaining feasible across a wide range of IoT hardware classes. © 2026 IEEE.
