AB  - This paper presents a novel lightweight encryption algorithm tailored for resource-constrained environments such as IoT and edge computing devices, with strong resilience against quantum computing threats. The proposed design integrates a hybrid cryptographic framework combining a post-quantum lattice-based key derivation mechanism with an adaptive symmetric encryption engine utilizing dynamic substitution-permutation logic. Experimental evaluations conducted on devices such as Raspberry Pi 4 and ESP32 demonstrate an encryption time of 1.82 s for 1 MB files—offering a 37% speed improvement over RSA-2048, with memory usage 60% lower. The system achieves a near-perfect Shannon entropy score of 7.98, confirming ciphertext unpredictability. In addition to benchmark comparisons with AES-256 and RSA, the analysis includes resilience against side-channel, fault-injection, and bit-flipping attacks. The source code has been made publicly available to support reproducibility and external validation. Overall, the proposed algorithm achieves a practical balance between cryptographic strength, quantum resistance, and efficiency, offering a forward-compatible solution for embedded and distributed systems. Results of all performance tests can be seen in Table 3. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
