AB  - With the accelerated integration of information technology and intelligence into power systems, security concerns have become increasingly prominent, especially when faced with complex power operations and ever-evolving security threats. This paper proposes a short-lifecycle authorization code generation and distribution protocol based on Physical Unclonable Functions (PUF) and quantum-resistant signature technology to address the challenges of authorization and authentication in power systems. The protocol combines hardware physical characteristics, short-lifecycle authorization codes, and quantum-resistant signature techniques to enhance both security and efficiency. A unique key seed is generated through the SRAM PUF of the ESP32 terminal, then a dynamic authorization code is created using the Keccak-256 algorithm, with the CRYSTALS-Dilithium signature algorithm further strengthening quantum resistance. Experimental results show that the protocol offers robust security and performance in power systems. For example, during the three-party interaction process of authorization code generation and verification, the system achieves an average response delay of 125 ms even under high concurrency, effectively preventing replay attacks, forgery, and man-in-the-middle attacks. The protocol consumes significantly fewer resources than traditional RSA-2048 schemes, making it especially suitable for resource-constrained power terminal devices. In summary, this protocol not only meets the real-time and security requirements of power systems but also offers high resource efficiency, providing an effective solution for high-security scenarios like smart grids. © 2025 IEEE.
