AD  - Department of Computer Science & Engineering, Islamic University, Kushtia, 7003, Bangladesh
AD  - Department of Computer Science & Engineering, Bangladesh University of Business and Technology, Dhaka, 1216, Bangladesh
AB  - The Internet of Things (IoT) connects everyday physical devices to the Internet to collect and exchange information anytime, from anywhere. Its adoption is increasing across domains such as healthcare, agriculture, and transportation. However, as IoT systems increasingly handle sensitive data, ensuring secure communication has become a critical challenge, particularly in device-to-device (D2D) communication, where devices interact directly without intermediaries. Traditional centralized models introduce single points of failure, making communication vulnerable if the central authority becomes unavailable. Moreover, mutual authentication in D2D communication is challenging because IoT devices often lack the resources to store and process credentials for all trusted devices. Existing solutions frequently rely on certificate-based infrastructure or specialized hardware (e.g., Physically Unclonable Functions), which impedes large-scale adoption. To address these challenges, this paper proposes BEdX25519, a lightweight blockchain-based authenticated key-exchange protocol for secure IoT D2D communication without any intermediaries. The protocol stores device identifiers and public keys on a decentralized and immutable blockchain ledger, preventing unauthorized tampering. Devices retrieve public keys directly from the blockchain and establish secure session keys using elliptic curve cryptography. The protocol is implemented on ESP32 devices to evaluate feasibility and performance. Experimental evaluations show that BEdX25519 is functional and efficient, with low execution time and memory usage on resource-constrained devices. Its security properties are validated through informal analysis and formal verification using the Scyther tool, and real-world attack simulations demonstrate resistance to replay, impersonation, and man-in-the-middle attacks. Additionally, blockchain transaction costs are evaluated on both Ethereum and Polygon networks, confirming cost-effectiveness. Finally, a large-scale deployment roadmap demonstrates that the protocol can support thousands of IoT devices without additional resource overhead, highlighting its practicality and scalability. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
