AB  - The rapid expansion of Internet of Things (IoT) applications across domains such as healthcare, smart cities, industry, and agriculture has intensified the need for secure yet lightweight cryptographic mechanisms. Conventional algorithms are often unsuitable for resource-constrained devices due to high computational, memory, and energy demands. This study presents a systematic review and performance evaluation of 39 lightweight cryptographic algorithms (LWC) implemented on the ESP32 platform. Key decision criteria - including energy efficiency, latency, memory footprint, and implementation cost - are analyzed. A multi-criteria decision-making framework using the TOPSIS method ranks algorithms based on performance tradeoffs. Results identify XTEA, SPECK, and TEA as top-performing candidates. The proposed framework serves as a practical guide for selecting efficient and secure LWC solutions in constrained IoT environments. © 2025 IEEE.
