AB  - The Internet of Things (IoT) requires lightweight cryptographic schemes that balance data security and resource efficiency. This study experimentally evaluates three modern lightweight encryption algorithms, namely ASCON, ACORN, and ChaChaPoly, implemented on ESP32 microcontrollers via UART communication. Each algorithm was tested over 31 runs to obtain statistically reliable results, including standard deviation (SD) and 95% confidence intervals (CI). The evaluation focused on processing speed, memory usage, CPU load, Bit Error Rate (BER), and diffusion percentage. ASCON achieved the fastest performance with mean encryption and decryption times of 53.26 μs and 54.58 μs, respectively, and the lowest CPU utilization at 0.47% during encryption and 1.97% during decryption. ACORN showed moderate latency of 63.00 μs and 64.00 μs but achieved the highest diffusion rate of 54.17 %, indicating stronger resistance to differential and linear attacks. ChaChaPoly exhibited balanced performance with encryption and decryption times of 66.03 μs and 69.33 μs, and a 0.00% BER, supported by integrated Poly1305 authentication. All algorithms maintained total memory usage between 582 kB and 586 kB, confirming efficient implementation within the ESP32 520 kB RAM constraint. These findings demonstrate that ASCON is optimal for low-latency, energy-efficient applications, ACORN is preferable for high-diffusion secure systems, and ChaChaPoly is suitable for general-purpose authenticated ToT communication. © 2025 IEEE.
