AB  - The rapid advancement of quantum computing poses a significant threat to the security of classical public-key cryptographic systems, driving the need for the adoption of post-quantum cryptographic (PQC) solutions. Digital signatures are a fundamental security primitive in Internet of Things (IoT) systems, providing authentication, data integrity, and secure firmware update mechanisms. However, deploying PQC digital signature schemes on resource-constrained IoT devices remains a major challenge due to their limited computational power and memory capacity. This paper presents an experimental benchmarking study of post-quantum digital signature schemes on two widely used embedded platforms: the ESP32 and the Raspberry Pi 4. The evaluation is conducted under realistic operating conditions using Universal Asynchronous Receiver/Transmitter (UART) and Message Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT) communication protocols, with all measurements obtained from real hardware implementations. Our results reveal critical trade-offs among execution time, memory footprint, and security level, and offer practical insights to guide the selection of suitable PQC digital signature schemes for deployment in constrained IoT environments. © 2026 IEEE.
